  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 307 AM CDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nSurface cyclone has pushed southeast of the area while surface high \npressure centered over the Upper Midwest spreads across much of the \ncentral CONUS. While there is not a lot of forcing at the surface, \nan H5 trough is moving across the Central Plains which will inhibit \nmid-level height rises. This will be just enough lift today to \nmaintain cloud cover for most of the morning and afternoon hours. \nThis may also help the development of light rainfall activity, \ndrizzle, or sprinkles across the area especially prior to noon \ntoday. However, temperatures will remain notably cooler throughout \neastern Kansas and Missouri today. If there is any type of light \nrain or drizzle activity today, this is expected to diminish but low-\nlevel moisture may keep lower-level clouds in play through the \nevening. \n\nTuesday and Wednesday, H5 ridge axis amplifies over the Front Range \nand High Plains, and will provide height rises into the lower \nMissouri River Valley and allow the surface high pressure to expand \nacross much of the region. This will help keep conditions mostly dry \nthrough the middle of this week. Low-level flow will generally be \nnortheasterly to easterly, limiting any notable temperature \nadvection regimes. Therefore, will maintain high temperatures in \nupper 60s for areas along Hwy. 36 and northward, with lower 70s \nsouthward. Overnight lows will dip into the lower 50s across much of \nthe area, with locations near the Iowa border potentially dropping \ninto the upper 40s. \n\nThursday and Friday, troughing over the Pacific Northwest will work \nto de-amplify the mid-level ridge pattern across the central CONUS, \nsending a series of vort maxima toward the Upper Midwest and lower \nMissouri River Valley. A weak thermal ridge should push eastward, \nand provide modest warm air advection that aids temperatures getting \nback into the mid 70s toward the end of the week. This will also \nprovide our next opportunity for rain shower activity. Current \nensemble guidance favors western Missouri and eastern Kansas for \nactivity starting late Thursday and continuing into Friday with 30-\n40 percent probabilities for measurable rainfall. This then expands \neastward into Central Missouri through the remainder of the weekend. \nSome model guidance does attempt to push temperatures back into the \n80s by the end of next weekend, but cloud cover and rain shower \npotential may largely limit that potential, and current spread \namongst ensemble guidance is fairly large for temperature forecasts \nnext weekend.",
